# Class -> Rust path glob (relative to the audited crate's `src`) map for scripts/audit-port.sh's
# per-class matching. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored. A class whose Rust home
# spans more than one file gets one map line per file — not one line with several space-separated
# globs, which the reader (`read -r cls path`) would only keep the first token of.
#
# This map discharges the Plan 3 obligation recorded in `docs/java-quirks.md`'s obligation
# register ("`audit-port.sh`'s positive match is crate-wide") for `fr-board`, which Plan 3 built
# the mechanism for and Plan 6 Task 18 applies (plan-6 ruling 13). Plan 2's nine directories now
# run under the 4-argument form:
#
#   for dir in board/model/items board/model/structure board/facade board/searchtree \
#              board/trace board/state rules core/library datastructures; do
#     ./scripts/audit-port.sh "$dir" crates/fr-board/src '*.java' scripts/audit-map/fr-board.map
#   done
#
# The 3-argument (crate-wide) form still exits 0 and is kept in `crates/fr-board/README.md` for
# comparison, but it is the weaker check: for a Java `Foo.getBar` it accepts any `fn get_bar…`
# anywhere in the crate. Under this map a class listed below has its methods and markers searched
# **only** under its mapped path(s); a class this file does not mention falls back to the
# crate-wide search *and* prints `UNMAPPED`, so the map cannot silently rot.
#
# Reading the globs. `Board` is one Rust type assembled from the **eight** `board/facade` classes
# (plan-2 ruling 1), and its inherent impls are split across `board/*.rs` by subject; those eight
# classes therefore map to `board/*.rs` rather than to one file, as does `Item` (nine rows in all). That is the "a class with no single home gets a glob"
# case the script's header allows, and it is a real narrowing all the same: it excludes
# `items/`, `rules/`, `library/`, `structure/`, `searchtree/` and `datastructures/`.

# --- board/model/items -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Java's `Item` hierarchy is one Rust enum plus a shared header (plan-2 ruling 2), and the methods
# each subclass declares land in three places: the item modules (`items/*.rs`), the `Board` impls
# that answer connectivity and clearance questions about an item (`board/connectivity.rs`,
# `board/clearance.rs`, `board/query.rs`), and `board/mod.rs` for the ones that mutate the board.
BoardItemType            items/mod.rs
PrintableShape           items/mod.rs
Connectable              items/*.rs
Connectable              board/connectivity.rs
Item                     items/*.rs
Item                     board/*.rs
ObstacleArea             items/*.rs
ComponentObstacleArea    items/*.rs
ComponentOutline         items/*.rs
ConductionArea           items/*.rs
ConductionArea           board/connectivity.rs
ViaObstacleArea          items/*.rs
DrillItem                items/*.rs
DrillItem                board/connectivity.rs
Pin                      items/*.rs
Pin                      board/connectivity.rs
Via                      items/*.rs
Via                      board/connectivity.rs
Via                      board/clearance.rs
Trace                    items/*.rs
Trace                    board/connectivity.rs
Trace                    board/mod.rs

# --- board/model/structure ---------------------------------------------------------------------
